---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import KeyFacts from '../../components/KeyFacts.astro';
import { techArticleSchema } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';
import SourcesStandards from '../../components/SourcesStandards.astro';
import RigFloorToolsDiagram from '../../components/RigFloorToolsDiagram.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: "What is a safety clamp, and when is it used?",
    a: "A safety clamp is a mechanical clamp fitted around the pipe body just below a tool joint, acting as a backup that stops the string from dropping if the elevators or the spider lose their grip unexpectedly. It carries no load during normal operation — tongs, slips and elevators do the actual work described on this page — but it's standard practice on many rigs during specific higher-risk moments, such as handling a single joint on the catwalk or running casing, where an unplanned drop has a worse consequence than the extra step of setting the clamp costs.",
  },
  {
    q: "What's the real difference between manual tongs and an iron roughneck?",
    a: "Manual tongs rely on the crew to apply torque by pulling a tong line wrapped around the cathead, with a second set of tongs holding the lower joint stationary — the torque value depends on crew technique and feel. An iron roughneck is a single hydraulic unit that spins the connection up and then applies a precise, pre-set torque value automatically, removing both the manual pulling and the judgment call about how much torque was actually applied. The mechanical job is identical either way; what changes is consistency and where crew members' hands are during the highest pinch-point moment of the cycle.",
  },
  {
    q: "What are the different elevator types, and why does it matter which one's on the rig?",
    a: "The common types are side-door elevators (hinge open from the side, the most common general-purpose type), center-latch elevators (latch at the front center, often used on larger or heavier strings), slip-type elevators (grip the pipe body directly with internal slips rather than latching below a tool joint upset, used where the connection geometry doesn't suit a standard latch), and single-joint elevators (sized for handling one joint at a time rather than a full stand, used on the catwalk or for specialty single-joint operations). Each is rated and sized for a specific pipe range and load — running the wrong elevator type for the string on hand is a real handling hazard, not just a mismatch in convenience.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why do slips have to be sized to the exact pipe diameter rather than a general range?",
    a: "A slip grips through wedging action — the tapered segments bite harder as the string's weight pulls down — and that mechanism only works correctly when the segments match the pipe's actual outside diameter closely. A slip sized too large won't seat and bite correctly; one sized too small can damage the pipe body or fail to support the rated load. It's the same sizing discipline the <a href=\"/equipment/bop#rams\">pipe rams on a BOP stack</a> require for the same underlying reason — a mechanical grip built around one specific diameter doesn't work as a loose fit for a nearby size.",
  },
];

const techArticleJsonLd = techArticleSchema({
  sourcePath: 'src/pages/equipment/rig-floor-tools.astro',
  path: '/equipment/rig-floor-tools',
  headline: "Rig Floor Hand Tools: Tongs, Slips, Elevators and Spinner Explained",
  description: "The core hand tools used to make up and break out drill pipe connections on the rig floor — tongs, iron roughneck, slips, elevators, and the spinning wrench — what each does and how they work together.",
  lang: 'en',
});
---
<BaseLayout
  extraJsonLd={[techArticleJsonLd]}
  title="Rig Floor Hand Tools: Tongs, Slips, Elevators and Spinner"
  description="The core hand tools for making up and breaking out drill pipe connections — tongs, iron roughneck, slips, elevators and the spinning wrench — explained."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Equipment', href: '/equipment' }, { label: 'Rig Floor Tools', href: '/equipment/rig-floor-tools' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/equipment/rig-floor-tools"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/equipment/rig-floor-tools" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Equipment — Rig Floor</div>
    <h1>Rig Floor <span>Hand Tools</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Every connection made or broken on the drill floor runs through the same handful of tools. Here's what each one does, and how a roughneck crew actually uses them together to run pipe.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-theyre-for">What They're For</h2>
        <p>Rig floor tools are the hand tools and mechanized units a crew uses to grip, spin, torque and support drill pipe every time a connection is made up or broken out while running a string in or out of the hole. Running a string in or out of the hole means making up or breaking out a threaded connection every single stand, hundreds of times over the life of a well. The tools on this page are what a <a href="/careers/roughneck">roughneck</a> crew uses to grip, spin, torque, and support pipe safely through that cycle — none of them do anything on their own; they work in a fixed sequence every time a connection is handled.</p>
        <RigFloorToolsDiagram lang="en" />

        <KeyFacts
          lang="en"
          rows={[["Core tools", "Tongs (or iron roughneck), slips, elevators, spinning wrench or kelly spinner."], ["Primary job", "Grip, spin, torque and support pipe during every connection made up or broken out."], ["Who operates them", "The rig floor crew (roughnecks), under the driller's direction from the console."], ["Manual vs mechanized", "Manual tongs and slips are the traditional setup; an iron roughneck mechanizes make-up/break-out and has become standard on most modern rigs."]]}
        />

        <h2 id="tongs">Tongs</h2>
        <p>Tongs grip the pipe body and apply the rotational torque that makes up or breaks out a threaded connection. Traditional rig floors use two sets — a pair of manual tongs, one holding the lower joint stationary while the other applies torque to the upper joint — operated by hand with the crew pulling a tong line — a cable wrapped around the cathead, a rotating spool on the drawworks that supplies the pulling force — for leverage. This is physically demanding, repetitive work, and it's one of the main reasons a roughneck's early months are as much about building the specific strength and technique this job requires as learning the equipment itself.</p>
        <p>An <strong>iron roughneck</strong> mechanizes the same job: a single hydraulically powered unit that spins the pipe up and then applies precise, measured torque to the exact value the connection calls for, without a crew member physically pulling tong lines. It's become the standard on most modern land and offshore rigs because it's both faster and safer — torque is applied consistently to spec rather than by feel, and it removes hands from the highest pinch-point risk area on the floor during make-up and break-out.</p>

        <h2 id="slips">Slips</h2>
        <p>Slips are wedge-shaped, toothed inserts that sit in the rotary table (or in a casing/tubing spider for those strings) and grip the pipe body to hold the full weight of the string hanging in the hole once it's released from the elevators. They work purely on wedging action: the weight of the string pulling down drives the tapered slip segments tighter against the pipe, which means the grip gets stronger exactly when it needs to, under load — not something that can loosen on its own mid-connection.</p>
        <p>Slips are sized to the specific pipe diameter and weight being run, the same way a <a href="/equipment/bop#rams">pipe ram</a> is sized to one pipe diameter — a mismatched slip either won't seat correctly or won't support the load it's rated for, which is a serious handling hazard rather than a minor inconvenience.</p>

        <h2 id="elevators">Elevators</h2>
        <p>Elevators are the hinged, clamping assembly that latches around the pipe just below a tool joint's upset (the thicker shoulder at each pipe end) and connects up to the traveling block via bails, letting the hoisting system lift and lower the entire string. Where slips hold the string stationary in the rotary table, elevators are what actually move it — raising a new stand into position to make up, or lowering the string to set it back in the slips once a connection is broken.</p>
        <p>The handoff between the two is the core rhythm of tripping pipe: elevators lower the string until its weight transfers onto the slips, the connection gets broken or made up, then the elevators latch the next joint and the cycle repeats. Every trip in or out of the hole is this same sequence, run however many times the string is long.</p>
        <p>Elevators come in several distinct types, matched to the string and the job rather than interchangeable:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Side-door elevators</strong> hinge open from the side to latch around the pipe — the most common general-purpose type on a drill pipe string.</li>
          <li><strong>Center-latch elevators</strong> latch at the front center of the body instead, a design more commonly suited to larger or heavier strings where a side-door's hinge geometry is less practical.</li>
          <li><strong>Slip-type elevators</strong> grip the pipe body directly with internal slip segments rather than latching below a tool joint's upset, used where the connection geometry doesn't suit a standard latch — on tubing or certain workover strings, for example.</li>
          <li><strong>Single-joint elevators</strong> are sized to handle one joint at a time rather than a full multi-joint stand, used on the catwalk or for specialty single-joint handling rather than main tripping operations.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Like slips, every elevator type is rated and sized to a specific pipe range and load — running the wrong type for the string on hand is a handling hazard, not a convenience trade-off.</p>

        <h2 id="safety-clamps">Safety Clamps</h2>
        <p>A safety clamp is a mechanical clamp fitted around the pipe body just below a tool joint, carrying no load during normal operation but ready to stop the string from dropping if the elevators or a spider lose their grip unexpectedly. It's a backup, not a primary tool — tongs, slips and elevators do the actual gripping, spinning and supporting work described above — but it's standard practice on many rigs at specific higher-risk moments: handling a single joint on the catwalk, running casing, or any operation where an unplanned drop has a worse consequence than the extra step of setting the clamp costs. Treat a safety clamp as cheap insurance against a specific failure mode, not as redundant equipment that slows the job down for no reason.</p>

        <h2 id="power-slips-iron-roughneck">Power Slips and the Iron Roughneck</h2>
        <p><strong>Power slips</strong> are hydraulically actuated slips that set and release automatically rather than requiring a crew member to physically set and knock out manual slips by hand at the rotary table. They remove one more set of hands from the pinch-point zone around the rotary table during every connection cycle, the same safety logic behind the iron roughneck's mechanization of tongs. On a modern rig equipped with both power slips and an iron roughneck, a connection can be made up or broken out with the crew's hands clear of the rotating and gripping components for almost the entire cycle — manual tongs and manual slips both still exist and both still work the same mechanical principle described above, but the trend on new-build rigs is toward mechanizing every part of this cycle that doesn't strictly require a human hand on the pipe.</p>

        <h2 id="spinning-wrench">Spinning Wrench and Kelly Spinner</h2>
        <p>Before tongs can apply real torque, a connection has to be spun together by hand-tight engagement first — threading the joint far enough that the tong can then apply the final, measured torque without cross-threading. A <strong>spinning wrench</strong> (or power tong set to spin mode on modern rigs) does this fast, hydraulically powered spin-up, replacing the older method of spinning pipe by hand with a chain tong.</p>
        <p>A <strong>kelly spinner</strong> is a related but distinct tool found on rigs still using a conventional kelly and rotary table rather than a top drive — it's mounted at the top of the kelly itself and spins the kelly into the connection below it. On a <a href="/equipment/rotating-system">top drive rig</a>, the top drive's own motor performs this function directly, which is one of several reasons top drive rigs need fewer separate floor tools than a conventional kelly rig does.</p>

        <h2 id="tool-comparison">Tool Comparison at a Glance</h2>
        <p>The same facts from above, side by side — what each tool does, its mechanized equivalent where one exists, and which standard above actually names it. A dash means the standard above doesn't list that specific tool by name, not that no standard exists anywhere.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap table-scroll-hint" role="region" aria-labelledby="tool-comparison" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Primary Job</th><th>Mechanized Equivalent</th><th>Named In</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Tongs</td><td>Grips the pipe and applies rotational torque to make up or break out a connection</td><td>Iron roughneck</td><td>API Spec 7K</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Slips</td><td>Wedge-grips the pipe body to hold the string's weight in the rotary table</td><td>Power slips</td><td>API Spec 7K</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Elevators</td><td>Latches below a tool joint's upset and connects to the traveling block to raise/lower the string</td><td>—</td><td>API Spec 8C</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Spinning wrench</td><td>Spins a connection hand-tight before tongs apply final torque</td><td>Top drive (on top drive rigs)</td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Kelly spinner</td><td>Spins the kelly into the connection on a conventional kelly rig</td><td>Top drive (replaces it entirely)</td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Safety clamp</td><td>Backup clamp, stops the string dropping if elevators or a spider lose grip</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="how-they-work-together">How They Work Together: Making Up a Connection</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>The string already sits in the slips</strong>, set in the rotary table holding its full weight from the previous cycle.</li>
          <li><strong>Elevators lower the new stand</strong>, aligning the pin end with the box end of the stationary joint held in the slips below.</li>
          <li><strong>Spinning wrench or iron roughneck spin tool engages</strong>, threading the connection hand-tight without cross-threading.</li>
          <li><strong>Tongs (or the iron roughneck's torque function) apply final make-up torque</strong> to the exact value specified for that connection — under-torque risks the joint backing off downhole, over-torque risks damaging the threads. The string stays supported in the slips throughout this entire step.</li>
          <li><strong>Elevators take the string's weight</strong> and the slips release, letting the now-extended string lower further into the hole.</li>
          <li><strong>Slips reset around the string</strong> at its new position; elevators release and move up to latch the next stand, and the cycle repeats.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Breaking out a connection while tripping out of the hole runs the identical sequence in reverse. Every one of these tools exists because a connection has to be gripped, spun, torqued, and supported as four genuinely separate mechanical jobs — no single tool on the floor does more than one of them.</p>

        <div class="note-box">
          <strong>Reference only.</strong> Torque specifications, slip ratings, and elevator capacities vary by manufacturer, pipe size, and connection type — always confirm against your rig's actual torque chart and equipment spec sheets before relying on any figure for a real job.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
